      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are 6-point or 12-point sockets better?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Six-point for grip, twelve-point for access. Six-point sockets contact the flats of the fastener, spreading load over the strongest part of the head, which is what you need on seized, high-torque or soft fasteners. Twelve-point sockets engage every 30 degrees instead of 60, which matters when something blocks the swing of your ratchet."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do 12-point sockets round bolts?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "They make it far more likely. Their narrower contact areas bear close to the corners of the head, where the metal deforms first, so on a fastener needing more than its design torque the corners give way and the socket slips. Each slip makes the next attempt worse. On seized bolts, use six-point."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "When should I use a 12-point socket?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "When access is the problem rather than the fastener: restricted ratchet swing in an engine bay, working blind by feel, or removing many lightly torqued bolts quickly. Also whenever the fastener is genuinely a 12-point (double hex) or square head, where it is the only correct tool."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What socket should I buy first?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A six-point set. It handles the overwhelming majority of fasteners safely, and access problems are better solved with extensions, wobble extensions and a universal joint than with twelve-point sockets. Add twelve-point later if your work regularly involves tight engine bays."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Why are impact sockets 6-point?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Because hammering torque onto the corners of a fastener is the quickest way to round it. Impact wrenches deliver exactly that kind of load, so almost all impact sockets are six-point. Twelve-point impact sockets exist but are intended for genuine twelve-point fasteners, not general use."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can a 12-point socket fit a square bolt head?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, and this is one of its genuine advantages. The twelve positions engage all four corners of a square head cleanly, which is why plumbers and people working on older machinery keep a twelve-point set even when they use six-point for everything else."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do I remove a bolt I have already rounded?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Try tapping a six-point socket one size down onto the head — a 12mm onto a rounded 13mm often bites into what remains of the flats. Failing that, a fluted bolt extractor socket works but usually destroys the fastener. Heat followed by penetrating oil lowers the torque needed and makes a marginal grip succeed."
